# The Google Pixel 3a (sargo), and the only place in the project that names it
# (docs/devices.md D1, D2).
#
# The second device, and the one the abstraction was built for. Everything
# here is either a dependency or a hardware value; not a line of shell, not a
# QML file, not a keybinding. If something device-shaped cannot be expressed in
# this package, the boundary is in the wrong place and devices.md §4 is the
# file to argue with.
#
# What is NOT here, and deliberately:
#
#   The boot image. A device package describes a running system; getting that
#   system onto flash is image/boot/android-bootimg.sh's job (D9). The two are
#   separate because the rootfs is device-independent (D8) and only the last
#   step is not.
#
#   Telephony. sargo's modem needs q6voiced and hexagonrpcd, neither of which
#   is packaged for Arch. DEVICE_SERVICES is empty and device.conf says so
#   rather than naming a unit that is not there -- moarchy-firstboot would warn
#   on every first boot for a feature nobody has built yet.

. "$startdir/../../scripts/manifest.sh"
_ver=$(manifest_get moarchy version) || return 1

pkgname=moarchy-device-sargo
pkgver=$_ver
# 2: gained a qbootctl dependency and the unit that marks the boot slot
# successful. The contents changed while pkgver did not, and pkgver comes from
# manifest.toml -- so without this bump docker/build-packages.sh keeps
# yesterday's file (it compares pkgver-pkgrel against PKGDEST) and a phone in
# the field sees no upgrade to take.
# 3: gained moarchy-qcom-modem, which is what gives this phone Wi-Fi (D27).
pkgrel=3
pkgdesc="Google Pixel 3a (sargo) hardware support for moarchy"
arch=('any')
url='https://github.com/SimonSchubert/moarchy'
license=('MIT')

# One device package at a time, enforced by pacman rather than by care (D4).
provides=('moarchy-device')
conflicts=('moarchy-device')

# linux-firmware-qcom carries qcom/a630_sqe.fw and qcom/a630_gmu.bin, the free
# Adreno microcode the GPU needs to come up at all.
#
# The SUBPACKAGE, and that is the whole point of this entry. Arch splits
# linux-firmware per vendor, and plain `linux-firmware` pulls in amd, amdgpu,
# atheros, broadcom, cirrus, intel, mediatek, nvidia, other, radeon, realtek,
# ti and whence -- every vendor except the one this phone is built on. The
# image had all thirteen and no qcom directory beyond what our own firmware
# package put there.
#
# What that cost: moarchy booted to a login on 2026-09-14 and sway died with
#   MESA: error: get_param:235: get-param failed: -6 (No such device or address)
#   [wlr] Failed to initialize EGL -> [sway/server.c:270] Failed to create renderer
# A phone that boots, autologins, and has no display server. The earlier
# `[drm:adreno_request_fw] *ERROR* failed to load qcom/a630_sqe.fw` seen under
# postmarketOS was the same missing file and was misread at the time as merely
# an artifact of their minimal initramfs.
#
# firmware-moarchy-sargo carries the proprietary half: the a615_zap shader the
# Adreno needs on top of that free microcode, plus adsp, cdsp, ipa_fws, venus,
# wlanmdsp and -- since 0.2.2-2 -- mba and modem. That is every path the device
# tree names, with nothing held back.
#
# moarchy-qcom-modem is Wi-Fi, which is not what its name suggests and is the
# reason that package's header is as long as it is. The short version: WCN3990's
# Wi-Fi firmware runs on the modem DSP, nothing in the kernel boots that DSP
# (`rproc->auto_boot = false`), and `rmtfs -s` is what does. Without this
# dependency the phone has a bound ath10k_snoc, a present board file, and no
# wlan0 -- which reads like a driver problem and is a missing daemon.
#
# linux-firmware-atheros is NOT named here and that is deliberate: `linux-firmware`
# already pulls it in (it is one of the thirteen vendor subpackages, unlike
# qcom), and it carries both ath10k/WCN3990/hw1.0/board-2.bin for Wi-Fi and
# qca/crbtfw21.tlv + qca/crnv21.bin for Bluetooth. Verified with `pacman -Fx`
# against the real database rather than read off the upstream git tree, which
# is the mistake that cost this device its GPU twice.
#
# qbootctl is what stops this phone bricking itself. An A/B bootloader counts
# down a retry counter on every handoff and marks the slot unbootable at zero
# unless the OS calls back; measured here as slot-retry-count:a:0 and
# slot-unbootable:a:yes, in which state the bootloader boots NOTHING -- a
# postmarketOS image that had worked an hour before failed the same way ours
# did (D26).
depends=(
  'linux-moarchy-sdm670'
  'linux-firmware'
  'linux-firmware-qcom'
  'firmware-moarchy-sargo'
  'moarchy-qcom-modem'
  'qbootctl'
)

source=('sway.conf' 'device.conf' 'qbootctl-mark-successful.service')
sha256sums=('SKIP' 'SKIP' 'SKIP')

package() {
  install -Dm644 "$srcdir/sway.conf"   "$pkgdir/usr/share/moarchy/device/sway.conf"
  install -Dm644 "$srcdir/device.conf" "$pkgdir/usr/share/moarchy/device/device.conf"

  printf 'codename=sargo\n' > "$srcdir/build-info"
  install -Dm644 "$srcdir/build-info" "$pkgdir/usr/share/moarchy/device/build-info"

  # Shipped ENABLED, by the symlink systemctl would create, rather than left to
  # moarchy-firstboot and DEVICE_SERVICES like the PinePhone's modem daemon.
  #
  # The difference is what failure costs. A modem that does not come up is a
  # phone with no calls; a slot that is never marked is a phone that stops
  # booting three reboots later, needing a computer with fastboot to revive.
  # DEVICE_SERVICES runs once, on the first boot, from a script that can fail
  # -- this must be true from the moment the package is installed, including in
  # an image that never completes a first boot.
  install -Dm644 "$srcdir/qbootctl-mark-successful.service" \
    "$pkgdir/usr/lib/systemd/system/qbootctl-mark-successful.service"
  install -d "$pkgdir/usr/lib/systemd/system/multi-user.target.wants"
  ln -s ../qbootctl-mark-successful.service \
    "$pkgdir/usr/lib/systemd/system/multi-user.target.wants/qbootctl-mark-successful.service"
}
